Below you’ll find two complete examples –  
* **Example A** uses the popular *async* NPM module (`async.queue`) which already implements everything you asked for.  
* **Example B** shows how to build your very own lightweight queue from scratch so you see exactly why “saturated” / “unsaturated” fire at those moments.

Both snippets run under Node ≥ 12 (or in modern browsers via bundlers). Pick whichever fits your project style.



--------------------------------------------------------------------

### Example A – Using `async.queue`

```js
// Install first:
//   npm install async          # or yarn add async

const async = require("async");

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Create a queue that runs no more than 5 concurrent workers.
// Each worker receives a single argument – our task data.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
const q = async.queue((taskData, done) => {
  // Your real work goes here. For demo purposes we just log:
  console.log(`[Worker] Processing task "${taskData}"`);
  // Simulate async I/O with a timeout
  setTimeout(done, Math.random() * 200 + 50);
}, /*concurrency*/ 5);

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Saturation callbacks ------------------------------------------------
// These fire automatically whenever the queue’s state changes.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
q.on("saturated", () => console.log("[Event] Queue is saturated"));
q.on("unsaturated", () => console.log("[Event] Queue is unsaturated"));

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Push some dummy tasks ---------------------------------------------
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
for (let i = 1; i <= 20; ++i) {
  q.push({ id: i }, err => {
    if (!err) console.log(`[Done] Finished task ${i}`);
  });
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Optionally wait until all queued jobs finish ---------------------
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
q.drain(() => console.log("[Drain] All tasks have been processed."));
```

#### What happens?

| State | Trigger |
|-------|---------|
| **Saturated** | When the number of currently executing workers reaches the concurrency limit (here 5). |
| **Unsaturated** | Whenever a worker finishes and the total falls below the limit again. |

Because we’re pushing 20 items into a pool of only five workers, you will immediately hit “Queue is saturated”, then as soon as a worker completes its job you’ll get “Queue is unsaturated”.  

Feel free to replace the body inside the worker function with whatever logic you actually want to perform per task.


--------------------------------------------------------------------

### Example B – A tiny hand‑rolled queue

If you prefer not to pull in an extra dependency, this minimal implementation gives you the same behaviour while keeping the source visible.

```js
/**
 * Simple FIFO task queue supporting concurrency limits and saturation events.
 */
class TaskQueue {
  constructor(concurrency) {
    this.concurrency = concurrency;
    this.activeCount = 0;
    this.taskBuffer = [];
    this.listeners = new Map();      // eventName → Set<callback>
  }

  /** Register listeners for 'saturated' & 'unsaturated' */
  on(event, cb) {
    if (!this.listeners.has(event)) this.listeners.set(event, new Set());
    this.listeners.get(event).add(cb);
  }
  off(event, cb) {
    const s = this.listeners.get(event);
    if (s) s.delete(cb);
  }
  _emit(event, ...args) {
    const s = this.listeners.get(event);
    if (s) for (const fn of s) fn(...args);
  }

  /**
   * Add a task to the queue.
   *
   * @param {(cb)=>void|string} taskFn Either a function `(done)` or a plain value.
   *                                   If it's a value, it'll simply be logged.
   */
  enqueue(taskFn) {
    this.taskBuffer.push(taskFn);
    this._maybeRun();
  }

  /** Try to start pending tasks respecting the concurrency cap. */
  _maybeRun() {
    while (
      this.activeCount < this.concurrency &&
      this.taskBuffer.length > 0
    ) {
      const nextTask = this.taskBuffer.shift();

      // Fire saturated once we reach capacity
      if (++this.activeCount === this.concurrency)
        this._emit("saturated");

      try {
        // Normalise to a function form
        let fn =
          typeof nextTask === "function"
            ? nextTask
            : (done) => {
                console.log(nextTask);
                done();
              };

        // Execute asynchronously to avoid blocking the loop
        Promise.resolve()
          .then(() => fn(this._createCallback()))
          .catch(err =>
            console.error(
              "[Error] Unhandled exception in task:",
              err.message || err
            )
          );
      } catch (ex) {
        console.warn("[Warning] Failed to schedule task:", ex);
        this._decrementActive();
      }
    }
  }

  /** Helper that returns a completion callback for a task. */
  _createCallback() {
    return (...results) => {
      this._decrementActive(results);
    };
  }

  /** Decrease active count and possibly trigger unsaturated. */
  _decrementActive(resultArgs) {
    --this.activeCount;
    if (this.activeCount < this.concurrency)
      this._emit("unsaturated");
    // You could forward resultArgs somewhere else if needed
    this._maybeRun(); // Start another waiting task if possible
  }
}

/**
 * Demo usage
 */

const myQ = new TaskQueue(5);

myQ.on("saturated", () => console.log("[Event] Queue is saturated"));
myQ.on("unsaturated", () => console.log("[Event] Queue is unsaturated"));

for (let i = 1; i <= 15; ++i) {
  myQ.enqueue({
    id: i,
    msg: `Hello world!`,
  }); // Plain objects become simple logs
}

console.log("[Info] Enqueued 15 tasks.");
```

#### How does it know when it’s saturated?

When `_activeCount` increments to equal the configured concurrency, the queue emits `"saturated"` right away. As soon as any worker calls back (via the returned callback), `_activeCount` drops below the limit and `"unsaturated"` fires.

This pattern mirrors the behavior of many production queues such as BullMQ, Kue, or even Redis Streams—only without the persistence layer.

---

## Quick checklist before deploying

| ✅ Item | Why it matters |
|--------|----------------|
| Use `process.nextTick()` or `setImmediate()` if you plan to do heavy CPU work inside a task, otherwise block the event loop. | Keeps other queued tasks responsive. |
| Wrap task bodies in `try/catch` or promise `.catch()` to prevent unhandled rejections crashing the process. | Robustness. |
| Consider adding a graceful shutdown hook (`SIGINT`, `SIGTERM`). | Ensure all queued tasks finish cleanly. |
| Monitor metrics (queue length, average latency) if scaling beyond trivial workloads. | Operational visibility. |

Happy queuing! 🚀

